easonable. Might be 3? \"some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\" Could be 3. Or 4 easy to implement at low cost? Requires senior engineer time, maybe not low cost. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Evidence: Requires senior engineering time and systematic mapping but uses existing staff and processes.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability; 3 reasonable and will contribute... partial solution; 2 unreasonable unlikely;1 solves unrelated problem\n\nGap analysis identifies where logging fails, allocate funding only to necessary updates. Will it solve logging mandate? It identifies gaps but does not implement logging. Problem is must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Audit helps allocate resources but doesn't actually implement logging. So contributes partially. Could be 3 reasonable and will contribute to solution. Or maybe 3.\n\nEvidence: It maps non-compliance but does not itself create logs, so it only informs remediation rather than fulfilling Article 12.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 solves identified problem directly related; 3 solves implied problem related; 2 may have some benefit... ;1 intervention not stated\n\nIt directly addresses how to meet logging mandate via gap analysis. That's directly related. So 4? It solves identified problem that is directly related: need to know where logging fails. Probably 4.\n\nEvidence: The audit directly targets Article 12 compliance gaps in existing prototypes, which is the core decision about meeting the logging mandate.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nProvide format maybe JSON? Instruction says give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably list.\n\nWe'll output:\n\nid: P2784\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality:1 etc.\n\nLet's produce clear.\n\n",
