        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P1617.\n\nProposal: Traceability by Design Certification Program. Internal professional development program trains researchers on documentation standards required by EU AI Act. Shifts focus from pure performance to combined performance and traceability metric. Implement mandatory monthly workshops focusing on technical documentation, versioning training data, legal necessity of event logs. Completion tied to project milestone approvals. Institutional survival depends...\n\nRate six subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal is training program to shift focus to performance+traceability metric. This changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations? It weaves logging into science via training and metric shift. Might be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). Not breaking? Breaking would change what lab researches or introduces new elements AND changes relationship... Might be slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Hmm shifts focus to combined metric, mandatory workshops tied to milestones. That's more than just tools. I'd say paradigm stretching: changes relationship, logging woven into science.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nTraining program for compliance is common. Might be common, mundane. Could be interesting but likely common. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring. Maybe 2 = interesting? Training for AI Act compliance is fairly standard expectation. Probably common.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill training solve logging mandate? It improves documentation and awareness, contributes to compliance but doesn't technically implement logs. Problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Training helps adapt R&D, contributes partially. So effectiveness 3? Could be 4? Will it solve problem without regard workability? Probably not fully solve technical logging requirement. It's partial. So 3.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nIt clearly applies to mandate: training on documentation standards, event logs. So 4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). Yes.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy low cost or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change expensive/difficult but not impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nMandatory monthly workshops tied to milestone approvals. That's reasonably feasible, some changes. Could be 3. Maybe 4? Easy to implement at low cost? Monthly workshops require staff time. Might be some changes. I'd say 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon unusual strategies do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 = radically violates\n\nTraining program is acceptable, common. 4.\n\nNow produce