ng woven into the science\" is example for 3. It does integrate into loops. I'd say 2? Let's think: standard approach is add logging after. This is middleware integrated. Could be slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nThe instrument wants one level per subscale. Probably paradigm_relatedness = 2 (slightly paradigm stretching). Because it introduces new elements tools but still compliance as logging practice, not changing relationship fundamentally.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare and ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nAutomated logging middleware is fairly common. Probably common/mundane. Could be interesting? In AI compliance, it's a typical solution. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution partial\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated\n\nIt automates event recording and traceability. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, enable recording relevant events etc. Middleware capturing metadata automatically seems effective. Would solve main problem? Might be sufficient. I'd say 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied...\nProposal directly addresses logging mandate. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, expensive/difficult but not impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDeveloping shared internal API integrated into all high-risk pipelines is significant change, requires engineering effort. Maybe some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3? \"Develop a shared internal API that resea