erves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nThis is introducing new tool library but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious, imaginative, surprising\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nUnified compliance logging library is common, mundane. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Might be 2? Many labs build shared libraries. I'd say common. So 1.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill it solve logging mandate? Provides automatic event recording, consistent logging of system usage and input data matches. Handles timestamps and database references. That addresses Article 12. Seems will solve problem. Might be 4? Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Yes.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2...\n1...\n\nIt directly addresses logging mandate. So 4.\n\nProvide output echoing id exactly.\n\nNeed format: probably each subscale evidence then score.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2767\n\nacceptability_evidence: ... etc.\n\nWe need one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nEnsure sentence ends with period.\n\nReturn rating.\n\nMake sure subscale names exact: acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, effectiveness, applicability\n\nProvide <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nPossibly JSON? The instruction says give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Might be plain text.\n\nI'll output structured list.\n\nLet's produce final answer.",
