ous or surprising. Score 1.\n\nparadigm_relatedness evidence: It adds a visualization/reporting tool but keeps compliance as a separate logging and reporting practice without changing how R&D relates to compliance obligations. Score 2.\n\neffectiveness evidence: The dashboard improves reporting and traceability of existing logs but does not create the automatic recording mandated by Article 12, so it only partially addresses the mandate. Score 3.\n\napplicability evidence: Converting raw logs into human-readable audit reports directly supports regulatory review and post-market monitoring required by Article 12. Score 4.\n\nacceptability evidence: Building an internal compliance dashboard is a standard, legally compliant practice that offends no norms or sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nimplementability evidence: Deploying ELK stack and mapping log fields to Article 12 requirements requires modest engineering effort and integration changes but is reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
