ngenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nUnified compliance logging middleware is fairly common practice - centralized logging, middleware. Not ingenious. Might be common, mundane. Could be interesting? Probably common, mundane. So 1.\n\nBut maybe it's interesting? Standardized software layer for compliance is typical. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related (do X to get Y, Y part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal directly addresses how to meet logging mandate. Do X to get logs captured automatically meeting Article 12. So applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nWill automatic capture across projects ensure compliance? It captures system events and input data. Article 12 requires logging of events relevant for risk, post-market monitoring, operation monitoring, and for point 1(a) Annex III specific items: period of use, reference database, input data matched, identification of natural persons. The middleware captures system events and input data across all high-risk projects. Might be sufficient? Possibly not fully guarantee all specifics, but reasonable and will solve main problem? Might be partial. It ensures every deployment meets Article 12 requirements without rebuilding logic. Claims ensures. If you could do it, would solve? Probably reasonable and will solve main problem. So 4? Maybe 3 because may need customization per project for specific logging items. But the proposal says ensures that every deployment meets Article 12 requirements. I