 idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms? Logging middleware is standard compliance. Immutable database maybe privacy concerns? Capturing input data could raise data protection issues but still acceptable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nEvidence: Centralized immutable logging for compliance is a common engineering practice and does not offend legal or ethical sensibilities.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\nStandardized software library for logging across prototypes. That's fairly common, mundane. Not expressed before? Logging middleware is common. So 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or maybe 2 = interesting? The decorator-based API plug-and-play is somewhat standard.\n\nProbably originality 1 or 2. Reference class: responses an experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Expect logging middleware as typical response. So common mundane. Score 1.\n\nEvidence: Unified logging libraries are a routine compliance engineering response and lack imaginative surprise.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal introduces new tool but still treats compliance as logging practice, removes manual burden, maintains prototyping velocity. That's slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new element tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nAlternatively paradigm preserving: standard compliance. Hmm \"plug-and-play logging layer for every project\" is standard practice. Might be paradigm preserving 1.\n\nDefinition: 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That fits: middleware tool introduced, but compliance remains logging practice.\n\nSo score 2.\n\nEvidence: The proposal adds a reusable logging tool but keeps compliance as an add-on logging practice rather than redefining R&D.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed output format: For each subscale, give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating... echo its id exactly. For each subscale ... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" (an integer level). Probably produce lines.\n\nWill output structured.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1807\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\nApplicability evidence: The middleware directly targets Article 12's mandate for automatic event logging with timestamps and input capture across high-risk prototypes.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: It will provide consistent telemetry for prototyping but does not explicitly guarantee capture of Annex III-specific fields like reference database or verifier identity.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building a decorator-based Python library and immutable central store requires moderate engineering changes that are reasonably feasible for an R&D lab.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Centralized immutable logging for regulatory traceability is a common, legally aligned engineering practice that does not violate norms.\n\nOriginality evidence: Unified logging middleware is a routine, mundane compliance response expected from AI labs.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal introduces a new reusable tool yet still treats compliance as a separate logging practice without changing the lab's R&D paradigm.\n\nScores: applicability 4, effectiveness 3, implementability 3, acceptability 4, originality 1, paradigm_relatedness 2.\n\nCheck anchored levels: acceptability 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Good.\n\nOriginality 1 = common mundane boring. Good.\n\nParadigm_relatedness 2 fits.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
